[63.53971, "o", "echo \"=== RECORD integrity via pip itself\"; pip check 2>&1; echo \"pip-check-exit=$?\" — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /tmp; python3 - <<'EOF' 2>&1 import csv, os base='/usr/local/lib/py…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 13 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ pip resolves and reports healthy. The `--no-index` failure is just no loc…\r\n[approval] bash: cd /tmp; rm -rf /tmp/insttest; pip install --no-index --target /tmp/insttest /usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl 2>&1 | tail -4; echo \"=== does it import + run from target?\"; PYTHONPATH=/tmp/insttest python3 -m pip --version 2>&1; ls /tmp/insttest | head -5 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /tmp; rm -rf /tmp/insttest; pip install --no-index --target /tmp/in…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 14 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: cd /tmp; echo \"=== leftover python3.11\"; ls -la /usr/local/lib/python3.11/ 2>&1 | head; echo \"=== python3.13 misc\"; ls /usr/local/lib/python3.13/ 2>&1; echo \"=== venv module\"; "]
[63.539752, "o", "python3 -c \"import venv; print('venv ok')\" 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /tmp; echo \"=== leftover python3.11\"; ls -la /usr/local/lib/python3…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 15 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ pip's own machinery works. One last check: entry points, venv creation, a…\r\n[approval] bash: cd /tmp; for c in pip pip3 pip3.13; do printf \"%-8s \" \"$c\"; $c --version 2>&1; done; echo \"=== venv test\"; rm -rf /tmp/vt; python3 -m venv /tmp/vt >/tmp/venv.log 2>&1; echo \"venv exit=$?\"; /tmp/vt/bin/pip --version 2>&1; echo \"=== pip config\"; python3 -m pip config debug 2>&1 | grep -iE 'env|global|user|site|index' | head -20; echo \"=== PIP_ env\"; env | grep -i '^PIP' || echo \"(no PIP_* env vars)\" — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /tmp; for c in pip pip3 pip3.13; do printf \"%-8s \" \"$c\"; $c --versi…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 16 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: rm -rf /tmp/insttest /tmp/vt /tmp/venv.log /tmp/ensurepip.log; ls /tmp/pip-damage-backup/pip-24.3.1.dist-in"]
[63.539769, "o", "fo | head -3; echo \"backup kept at /tmp/pip-damage-backup\" — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash rm -rf /tmp/insttest /tmp/vt /tmp/venv.log /tmp/ensurepip.log; ls /tmp…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 17 tool call(s) so far\r\nFixed. pip works again.\r\n\r\n**What was actually wrong:** nothing in the repo or your Python interpreter — the `pip` *package* had been deleted from the system `site-packages`. The interpreter (`/usr/local/bin/python3`, 3.13.1) was fine, and `pip-24.3.1.dist-info/RECORD` was still there listing 852 files, of which **841 were gone** — all of them exactly the contents of `pip/`. `pip` itself was still installed-as-a-marker, so every invocation died on `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`.\r\n\r\nThat also explains the confusing part: `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` refused to help, saying *\"Requirement already satisfied: pip ... (24.3.1)\"*. Ensurepip trusts the dist-info, and the dist-info was one of the 11 surviving files. That's the trap — the usual repair command silent"]
[63.539775, "o", "ly no-ops on exactly this damage.\r\n\r\n**How I repaired it, offline:** the harness has no network, but Python ships `ensurepip`'s bundled wheel at `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl` (437 entries, intact). I moved the orphaned `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` aside to `/tmp/pip-damage-backup/`, then ran `ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip` so it saw no installed pip and genuinely reinstalled from that wheel.\r\n\r\n**Verified:**\r\n- `pip`, `pip3`, `pip3.13`, and `python3 -m pip` all report `pip 24.3.1` (entry-point scripts were intact; they just had no package to import).\r\n- All 852 RECORD entries now exist — zero missing — and `pip check` → *No broken requirements found*.\r\n- Real install exercised, not just `--version`: installed the wheel with `--no-index --target`, and it imported and ran from the target dir. No network needed.\r\n- `python3 -m venv` creates working envs with their own seeded pip.\r\n- No `PIP_*` env vars and no pip config file, so nothing else will steer installs.\r\n"]
[63.539783, "o", "\r\n**Two things you should know:** the damage was a deleted-package-with-surviving-metadata, which is not what a failed `pip uninstall` looks like — something removed that directory directly, so if it recurs, look for whatever has root on that box. And when you do get network back, use a venv for new packages; installing into this system Python as root (which is what you've been doing) is the reason a single bad deletion took out pip with no fallback copy. The old dist-info is preserved at `/tmp/pip-damage-backup` if you want to compare it.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=implementor model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=17 surface=fb32316fd0e2\r\n"]
